      "worked": "Let $D$ be the foot of the perpendicular from $C$ to $AB$, and let $CD=h$. Since $AB$ runs east–west, $\\angle CAB=\\alpha$ and $\\angle ABC=\\beta$.\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\angle ACB&=180^\\circ-(\\alpha+\\beta)\\\\AC&=\\frac{h}{\\sin\\alpha}\\end{align*}$$\n\nApply the sine rule in $\\triangle ABC$, using $\\sin(180^\\circ-\\theta)=\\sin\\theta$:\n\n$$\\begin{align*}\\frac{AC}{\\sin\\beta}&=\\frac{d}{\\sin[180^\\circ-(\\alpha+\\beta)]}\\\\\\frac{h}{\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta}&=\\frac{d}{\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)}\\\\h&=\\frac{d\\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta}{\\sin(\\alpha+\\beta)}\\ \\mathrm{m}.\\end{align*}$$\n\nThe perpendicular distance is the shortest distance from the caller to the road.",
